---
// /privacy/: the privacy statement (night phase 16, E4; docs/POLICIES.md). Every personal datum the
// registry holds, where, why, for how long, who else processes it, and the rights over it — the
// private collection of authors' contact details included (CLAUDE.md). A draft for the owner's review.
// Static; science.css only.
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import PolicyDraft from "../components/PolicyDraft.astro";
import { SITE_NAME } from "../config";
---
<Base
  title="Privacy"
  description="Every personal datum the registry holds, why, for how long, who else processes it, and your rights over it."
  breadcrumb={[{ text: "Privacy" }]}
>
  <div class="policy">
    <h1>Privacy</h1>
    <PolicyDraft drafted="2026-09-29" />
    <p class="summary">
      What {SITE_NAME} holds about people, why, where, for how long, who else handles it, and how to exercise
      your rights. {SITE_NAME} never asks for, reads or stores the email address of anyone who uses it; it sends
      no email; it shows no advertising, sells nothing and tracks no one.
    </p>

    <h2 id="who">1. Who is responsible</h2>
    <p>
      {SITE_NAME} is run by one person, its owner, who is the data controller: [the owner's name and postal
      address — to be completed by the owner]. There is no email contact, on purpose: every question and request
      is made in the site (<a href="/data-rights/">your data rights</a>, <a href="/report/">a report</a>), and
      answered there.
    </p>

    <h2 id="accounts">2. If you have an account</h2>
    <p>You sign in with ORCID, GitHub or Google; {SITE_NAME} asks them for as little as they allow.</p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>What</th><th>Why</th><th>How long</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>A random account number; the name the provider gave (if any); your ORCID iD and your GitHub login, when you linked them; when the account was made</td><td>To know you when you come back, and to show your public handle beside what you write</td><td>Until the account is deleted (on request now; by yourself with phase 09)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Each linked sign-in: the provider and its account number for you (an ORCID iD, GitHub's numeric id, Google's opaque id)</td><td>To sign you in</td><td>As the account</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Your sessions: a fingerprint (SHA-256) of each browser's session, its dates, and a short hint such as "Firefox on macOS" (never the full browser string)</td><td>To keep you signed in, and to list your sessions</td><td>30 days after the last use</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Your roles (verified author of a paper, maintainer of a repository) and your claims, with the words you gave</td><td>To let you correct and validate what is yours</td><td>As the account</td></tr>
        <tr><td>What you ask of the registry: papers submitted, corrections, validations of a tracing map, removal requests, with your words (email addresses removed)</td><td>To do what you asked, and to show you where it stands</td><td>As the account; a removal request as long as its decision</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      No email address: ORCID and Google are asked only for an identifier ("openid"), GitHub for no permission at
      all. A provider's token is used during the sign-in only, and never stored.
    </p>

    <h2 id="github-side">3. On the GitHub side</h2>
    <p>
      Repositories live in their owners' own GitHub accounts, under GitHub's terms and GitHub's privacy statement.
      {SITE_NAME} keeps only its own layer:
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>What</th><th>Why</th><th>How long</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Public repositories linked or created through {SITE_NAME}, the papers they are linked to, and which account linked them</td><td>To show a paper's code and keep its tracing map</td><td>Until the repository is unlinked or deleted (30 days of grace)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>A log of what each account did (the kind of action, when, on which repository, its GitHub numeric id), never the content, never a token</td><td>The daily limits, security, and a person's public activity (their profile's calendar and timeline; never whom they blocked, their notifications or their profile edits)</td><td>For as long as the database lasts; a person's public activity shows the last year</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Research issues and their comments: the words, and who wrote them as they are named (a GitHub login, an ORCID iD or a name)</td><td>A paper's scientific conversation; they are public</td><td>Until deleted by their author or hidden by moderation</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Stars, star lists, follows and watches, your profile (the words you chose), notification states</td><td>What you asked for; public unless your profile is private (then only you see your stars, lists and follows)</td><td>Until you remove them; notifications and their states 3 months (saved ones kept)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>The events that feed notifications: who did what, on which repository or paper, and the GitHub logins a text mentions (at most 10)</td><td>Your notifications and your feed, computed when you read them</td><td>3 months</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Personal tokens of the public API: a fingerprint (SHA-256), their name, their permissions, the day of their last use — never the token</td><td>To let your scripts act as you</td><td>Until revoked; 30 days after they expire</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Webhooks: the address as you gave it (shown to you only), the events, and a log of each delivery (its status and duration, never the receiver's answer)</td><td>To send what you asked for</td><td>Until deleted; deliveries 7 days</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Commit statuses posted by outside services: their words and address, and the handle of who posted them</td><td>They are public, on the commit's page</td><td>As the repository</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Reports (who reported is never shown; without an account, nothing about the reporter is kept), moderation decisions, appeals, blocks with your note, interaction limits, data-rights requests</td><td>The rules, and your rights</td><td>Reports 1 year after their decision; decisions and their public notices kept; limits until they end; requests 3 years after their answer</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Signed out, a repository's page asks {SITE_NAME} nothing: your browser reads GitHub's public API and files
      directly, on your own quota, so GitHub sees your address as for any GitHub page. What the static pages
      show of the registry's layer (stars, profiles, research issues) is published each night as files of the
      site: private profiles and private lists are never in them.
    </p>

    <h2 id="catalogue">4. Researchers' data in the catalogue</h2>
    <p>
      The catalogue describes published papers with code: their titles, dates, journals, DOIs, their authors'
      names, ORCID iDs and affiliations as the publications and the scholarly databases give them (Crossref,
      Europe PMC, PubMed Central, OpenAlex), and links to the authors' code, with copies of scripts whose
      licence allows it (email addresses in the code are masked). This is public scholarly information, shown
      so that research can be checked and reused (legitimate interest); an author may correct or claim a
      record from its page, ask for a record or its copies to be removed (<a href="/removal/">the removal
      page</a>), or object to it (<a href="/data-rights/">your data rights</a>). The public copies of scripts
      are also published as the Hugging Face dataset OpenScientificCodeRegistry/Database.
    </p>
    <h3 id="contacts">The authors' contact details, kept privately</h3>
    <p>
      For each author of a paper in the catalogue, {SITE_NAME} also keeps, <strong>privately</strong>, the contact
      details the paper itself publishes: email address, given and family names, ORCID iD, organisation,
      postal address and affiliation, linked to the paper and its DOI. They come from the paper's full text and
      its Europe PMC record, and serve to cite the authors and to reach the authors of the code about their own
      paper. They are kept on the owner's computer and in a private Hugging Face dataset
      (OpenScientificCodeRegistry/Private, which the software refuses to publish unless it is private). They
      are never shown on the site, never in a public file, and never used for mass email: the registry does not
      send email. An author may ask to see, correct or erase theirs, or object to their keeping, on{" "}
      <a href="/data-rights/">the data-rights page</a> (signed in with the ORCID iD the paper names).
    </p>

    <h2 id="cookies">5. Cookies and your browser's storage</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>What it is</th><th>How long</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>__Host-oscr_session</code></td><td>Your session, when you sign in (a random value; the site keeps only its fingerprint)</td><td>30 days, extended while you use it</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>__Host-oscr_signed_in</code></td><td>"Signed in: yes", no identity, so that a signed-out page asks the server nothing</td><td>As the session</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>__Host-oscr_flow</code></td><td>The sign-in in progress (sealed: its state and proof key), between the site and ORCID, GitHub or Google</td><td>10 minutes</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>__Host-oscr_forge</code></td><td>One authorized action in progress (sealed), between the site and GitHub</td><td>10 minutes</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      All are strictly necessary, secure, and for this site only: no advertising, analytics or third-party
      cookie, so there is nothing to consent to. Your browser's own storage keeps, for you only and never sent:
      your display preferences (localStorage), the drafts of files you edit and pull-request reviews you have not
      sent (localStorage), and answers of GitHub's API for the tab (sessionStorage).
    </p>
    <p>
      The human check (Cloudflare Turnstile) on the forms that publish words runs in Cloudflare's own frame and
      handles what your browser sends to it under Cloudflare's privacy policy; {SITE_NAME} receives only whether
      it passed.
    </p>

    <h2 id="processors">6. Who else handles data</h2>
    <dl>
      <dt>Cloudflare</dt><dd>Hosts the site, its server code and its databases (Workers, D1), and runs the human check (Turnstile). It processes every visitor's address and requests to serve them.</dd>
      <dt>GitHub</dt><dd>Sign-in with GitHub, the registry's GitHub App, and the repositories themselves; your browser reads GitHub directly on repository pages.</dd>
      <dt>ORCID and Google</dt><dd>Sign-in only, with an identifier and no email address.</dd>
      <dt>Hugging Face</dt><dd>Stores the public dataset of script copies, and the private dataset of the authors' contact details.</dd>
      <dt>Zenodo</dt><dd>Gives a DOI to a tracing map an author validates, with that author's name and ORCID iD as creator, at their request.</dd>
      <dt>OpenAlex, Crossref, Europe PMC, PubMed Central</dt><dd>The scholarly databases the catalogue is built from; on a paper's page, your browser reads the paper's text from Europe PMC (or PubMed Central).</dd>
      <dt>Software Heritage</dt><dd>Archives a public repository when a person asks.</dd>
    </dl>
    <p>
      Some of these process data outside the European Union, notably in the United States. [To be completed by
      the owner: the safeguard each relies on — for example the standard contractual clauses of the provider's
      data-processing terms, or the EU–US Data Privacy Framework where the provider is certified.]
    </p>

    <h2 id="signals">7. Do Not Track, Global Privacy Control</h2>
    <p>
      {SITE_NAME} does not track visitors, sell or share data for advertising, or profile anyone: there is
      nothing for these signals to turn off, and a browser that sends them is treated exactly like one that does
      not.
    </p>

    <h2 id="children">8. Children</h2>
    <p>
      {SITE_NAME} is made for researchers and is not directed at children; accounts are not meant for anyone
      under 16. If we learn that a child's data is here, it is removed.
    </p>

    <h2 id="rights">9. Your rights</h2>
    <p>
      You may ask for a copy of your data (access), for it in a machine-readable form (portability), for a
      correction (rectification), for its erasure, for its use to stop (objection), or for it to be kept but not
      used while a question is settled (restriction). Ask on <a href="/data-rights/">the data-rights page</a>:
      being signed in proves who you are (with ORCID, GitHub or Google); for the data of a paper's author, sign
      in with the ORCID iD the paper names. The answer comes on your page <a href="/account/moderation/">“What of
      mine is hidden”</a>, never by email, within one month (two more months for a complex request, and we say
      why). Exporting and deleting your account yourself come with a later version (phase 09). You may also
      complain to your data-protection authority (in France, the CNIL).
    </p>

    <h2 id="changes">10. Changes</h2>
    <p>This statement carries its date. A change that matters is announced on the site before it applies.</p>
  </div>
</Base>
